WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:06.749
The SCP Foundation seeks out anomalies across
land, air, and sea, and they’ve encountered

00:00:06.749 --> 00:00:09.020
just about everything.

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:13.450
From roller-coaster cars rocketing through
the midwestern skies, to world-destroying

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:16.280
horrors deep underground in alternate dimensions.

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:21.470
So, it’s no surprise to hear they’ve happened
upon something right out of an old sailor’s

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:25.720
ghostly tale: An actual, real-life ghost ship.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:30.970
You can almost smell the salty sea air and
hear the eerie drone of a foghorn just thinking

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:31.970
about it.

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:35.500
But of course, with the SCP Foundation, it’s
never simple.

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:41.940
It’s never just a ghost ship, but something
more lurking beneath the surface of that chilly

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:43.050
sea.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:48.600
Several years ago in Arcachon Bay, on the
southwest coast of France, something unusual

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.350
began to happen.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:55.500
Between nautical twilight on one day and nautical
twilight on the next, a mysterious ship would

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:56.500
appear.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:00.079
It was a 19th-century brig, with two square-rigged
masts.

00:01:00.079 --> 00:01:05.680
It would materialize from nothing, simply
fading into existence and sailing around the

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:06.680
bay.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:10.841
The first sighting was reported by an elderly
man in a fishing boat, who stated that, as

00:01:10.841 --> 00:01:16.659
the ghostly ship drew closer to him, his own
vessel stopped responding to his control.

00:01:16.659 --> 00:01:20.750
He jerked the wheel back and forth, attempting
to steer away from the brig, but the boat

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:23.170
would not respond to his direction.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:28.080
Suddenly, as if yanked by an unseen force,
the fishing boat was pulled underwater all

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:29.159
at once.

00:01:29.159 --> 00:01:33.290
The fisherman, luckily, was able to grab a
life preserver and free himself from the boat.

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:37.790
He floated alone for hours until he was spotted
by another passing boat.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:42.970
He tried to tell them of the unusual ship
he had seen, and what had happened, but by

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:45.399
then the brig had disappeared.

00:01:45.399 --> 00:01:49.939
The ship continued to appear on a regular
basis, every time staying within a kilometer

00:01:49.939 --> 00:01:55.000
of its original manifestation point, always
at the same time of day.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.810
Its effect on nearby ships and vessels was
consistent too, unfortunately for anyone who

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:02.119
happened to be in the water at the same time.

00:02:02.119 --> 00:02:07.329
Wheels, pedals, and other methods of directional
movement would stop working, and the vessel

00:02:07.329 --> 00:02:09.390
would be pulled underwater.

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:12.760
Most of the time, the people on board drowned.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:17.690
After a pattern was established, and it became
clear that the ship was definitely anomalous,

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:20.450
the Foundation designated it SCP-5987.

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:26.650
A 3-kilometer radius was set up around the
ship, blocking off the sea and air traffic.

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:31.430
Unfortunately, this was not enough to prevent
people from entering the radius anyway.

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:35.739
As more and more civilians sighted the ship,
and the drownings became increasingly difficult

00:02:35.739 --> 00:02:39.940
to cover up, the Foundation decided to attempt
to neutralize the ship.

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:44.060
A task force was approved to search the area
for anything that could be used to put the

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:45.900
apparition to an end.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:51.220
As it turns out, sometimes the rules the old
ghost stories teach you are true: You need

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:54.830
to figure out the correct way to put an angry
ghost to rest.

00:02:54.830 --> 00:03:01.200
On January 1, 2019, the SCPS Submarine Nephthys
was deployed in the bay, with Captain Doumer

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:02.430
at its helm.

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:08.099
The submarine approached SCP-5987, and as
it entered its area, the communication system

00:03:08.099 --> 00:03:10.709
went down and the submarine was pulled underwater.

00:03:10.709 --> 00:03:15.670
SCP Foundation Command attempted to re-establish
communication with the submarine, and after

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:18.500
several tries, was able to get through.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:21.730
Captain Doumer reported that the submarine
was partially damaged from colliding with

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:24.910
the ocean floor, but was still safe and operational.

00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:29.150
The communication system had come back online
once the submarine had reached the bottom,

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:32.840
and the pull of SCP-5967 had ceased.

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:36.870
The research team deliberated over what their
next step would be, but their decision was

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:43.060
made for them when SCP-5987 retreated further
from the submarine and raised a white flag

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:44.519
from its mainmast.

00:03:44.519 --> 00:03:49.060
At this point, Nephthys was instructed to
proceed to the point where the brig had first

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:50.060
appeared.

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:54.150
The submarine reached the manifestation point,
where they spotted wooden and steel pieces

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:56.349
jutting out of the ocean floor.

00:03:56.349 --> 00:04:00.870
Noting that this was clear evidence of a shipwreck,
Nephthys switched to search mode, to focus

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:03.120
further on the small wooden wreck.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:07.329
Captain Doumer then deployed the diving team
to clear out the wreckage and inspect it for

00:04:07.329 --> 00:04:08.940
anything significant.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:13.610
Several skeletons were found, corroded by
the saltwater to varying degrees.

00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:18.850
As spectrally significant remains do not decay
or corrode, according to Foundation research,

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:23.010
the skeletons were deemed unimportant to the
mission and left where they were found.

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:27.940
One of the divers, Agent Lacroix, found a
carved figurehead, resembling a siren or a

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:29.350
mermaid, beneath the hull.

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:34.960
Unlike every other part of the ship, the figurehead
was not damaged at all, completely untouched

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:35.960
by the elements.

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:40.460
It was collected and brought aboard the submarine,
to be taken back to the Foundation for further

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:41.460
study.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:45.081
As the submarine returned to the surface,
and made its way back across the bay, the

00:04:45.081 --> 00:04:48.430
chime of a bell rang out across the water.

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:53.870
As the sound faded from the air, SCP-5987
faded from view.

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:58.920
The next day, when nautical twilight rolled
around, the brig was nowhere to be seen.

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:04.400
Removing the figurehead, whatever its significance
was, had somehow tamed the ghostly ship.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:06.440
But what was the ship?

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:07.770
Where did it come from?

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:10.410
What did the figurehead have to do with it
all?

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:17.750
The figurehead, designated SCP-5987-1, was
brought back to Foundation Area 34 and examined

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:19.980
by the Marine Studies Department.

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:23.010
After some time, they were able to trace its
origin.

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:28.240
SCP-5987-1 once sat on the front of the Duc
de Dantzig, a French privateer brig from the

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:33.750
early 19th century, though analysis of its
material indicates it was made 200 years before

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:35.690
the construction of the ship.

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:39.600
The figurehead has some unusual properties
that set it apart from the rest of the wreckage

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:40.990
found in the bay.

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:45.320
It is able to control all seafaring vessels
within a kilometer, sinking them or running

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:46.440
them aground.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:51.850
It was on the Duc de Dantzig when it sank
in 1811 after being burned at sea.

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:56.710
This experience was extreme enough that it
created a spectral imprint on the location.

00:05:56.710 --> 00:06:00.990
An old American Secure Containment Initiative
file provided additional insight into the

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:06.400
origins of SCP-5987, and the events leading
to its demise.

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:12.990
The file described Item 6789-AH, a humanoid
with possible ontokinetic properties, named

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:14.640
Francois Aregnaudeau.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:18.400
He was a French corsair, Captain of the Duc
de Dantzig.

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:22.310
A bounty of 10 thousand francs was placed
on his head by a group known as the American

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:27.781
Secure Containment Initiative - a precursor
to the SCP Foundation from the 19th century

00:06:27.781 --> 00:06:32.600
- with a bounty of an additional 100 francs
for each member of his crew.

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:37.050
According to the file, Aregnaudeau was believed
to be some sort of anomalous being.

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:40.500
This assumption was formed from observations
of the Captain physically controlling the

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:43.370
paths of other ships within his line of sight.

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:47.280
While standing on the bough of the Duc de
Dantzig, Aregnaudeau was able to manipulate

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:51.300
the riggings and sails of enemy ships in any
way he wanted.

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:54.930
Some of the ships were pulled underwater,
their crews drowned.

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:57.520
Others were dashed against rocks and destroyed.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:00.020
Some others were run aground and looted.

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:04.160
This ability allowed the Captain to rise through
the ranks of the French military, capturing

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:07.990
over 20 military and merchant ships in less
than 10 years.

00:07:07.990 --> 00:07:12.030
The Initiative planned to capture the Captain
when he disembarked his ship, and sink the

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:14.550
ship in order to prevent him from escaping
custody.

00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:19.180
However, before they could implement their
plan, his own crew beat them to it.

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:24.020
While the exact details of what happened are
unknown, an 1812 report published in a French

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:28.320
newspaper described an encounter between a
navy ship and the Duc de Dantzig.

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:32.440
They spotted the ship floating at sea, bobbing
on the waves and heading in no particular

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:33.440
direction.

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:37.090
Concerned, the navy crew determined the best
course of action was to reach out to the crew

00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:41.470
of the other ship and determine if they were
Frenchmen in need of assistance.

00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:46.020
As the Navy ship drew closer, the crew was
perplexed and worried by a heavy silence that

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:47.630
hung over the other ship.

00:07:47.630 --> 00:07:52.120
There was no noise coming from the deck, no
yells for help, no shouted orders.

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:54.770
Only the slap of the water on wood.

00:07:54.770 --> 00:07:57.060
The ship did not appear to be damaged at all,
either.

00:07:57.060 --> 00:08:01.030
There was no sign of cannon fire that might
have blown out a part of the ship, nor was

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:03.470
there any damage from a recent storm.

00:08:03.470 --> 00:08:07.770
There was nothing that should have been keeping
the ship from continuing on its course.

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:12.160
The navy ship came up beside the Duc de Dantzig,
with a group of sailors preparing to board

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:13.160
it.

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:15.870
As they climbed aboard, they were horrified
at what they found.

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:20.610
The deck was positively drenched in blood,
smeared across its surface as if the entire

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:25.210
crew of at least 50 men had emptied their
veins right onto it.

00:08:25.210 --> 00:08:29.640
As the navy men continued their search, the
source of the blood soon became entirely too

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:30.640
clear.

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:36.480
The ropes had been turned into makeshift nooses,
corpses hanging from them and swinging limply.

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:40.890
Some other bodies were strewn across the deck,
abdomens ripped open and their entrails yanked

00:08:40.890 --> 00:08:42.840
out, thrown to the side.

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:47.800
Others had their faces collapsed by cannonballs,
even though there were no signs of enemy fire.

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:53.260
A closer look revealed that the blasts had
come from their own cannons, turned on themselves,

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:56.110
aimed at their own faces, and fired.

00:08:56.110 --> 00:09:01.800
The Captain himself, reeking of death and
putrefaction, had been tied to the main mast.

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:05.320
Swords were stabbed through his hands and
his feet like a crucifixion.

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:09.480
The naval officers made their way to the hold,
where they found bloodied papers identifying

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:10.480
the ship.

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:14.680
They were uncertain what had happened, but
posited that the crew had gone mad while lost

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:17.640
at sea, turning on the captain and then each
other.

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:21.880
To be safe, they decided to set the ship on
fire and leave it there to sink to the bottom

00:09:21.880 --> 00:09:23.240
of a watery grave.

00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:28.470
With Captain Francois Aregnaudeau disposed
of, though not by them, the Initiative closed

00:09:28.470 --> 00:09:31.250
their case file on him and the story was forgotten.

00:09:31.250 --> 00:09:37.710
At least, it was until the 2019 mission to
investigate SCP-5987.

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:41.830
With the discovery of the figurehead from
the wreckage, a centuries-old mystery was

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:42.830
solved.

00:09:42.830 --> 00:09:46.590
According to the researchers tasked with examining
the figurehead, it resented having to do the

00:09:46.590 --> 00:09:51.760
work of war and destruction and disliked working
at the beck and call of the Duc de Dantzig

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:53.060
and its captain.

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:57.400
Somehow, it compelled the crew to destroy
each other and the captain, in an attempt

00:09:57.400 --> 00:09:58.400
to free itself.

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:03.000
When the naval officers discovered the carnage
and set the ship ablaze, it was traumatic

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:08.470
enough that whatever supernatural force powers
the figurehead left a spectral disturbance

00:10:08.470 --> 00:10:09.470
behind.

00:10:09.470 --> 00:10:14.010
The designation SCP-5987 was then created.

00:10:14.010 --> 00:10:18.440
SCP-5987-1 has been treated for saltwater
protection and attached to a historically

00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:21.460
accurate miniature 19th-century sailing ship.

00:10:21.460 --> 00:10:26.100
The ship, with the figurehead attached, is
kept in a saltwater enclosure in Area 34’s

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:27.590
Marine Observatory.

00:10:27.590 --> 00:10:31.110
It is checked once a month for any signs of
damage or degradation.

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:35.670
The figurehead uses its ability to control
ships to sail its new home around the saltwater

00:10:35.670 --> 00:10:36.720
enclosure.

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:41.390
It seems to enjoy spending its time this way,
and several of the officers assigned to guard

00:10:41.390 --> 00:10:47.330
the enclosure have reported hearing voices
singing the French sea shanty Boney (Was a

00:10:47.330 --> 00:10:48.330
Warrior).

00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:51.430
When the guards opened the door to look for
the source of the voices, however, they could

00:10:51.430 --> 00:10:53.080
not find a source.

00:10:53.080 --> 00:10:57.650
Somehow, the figurehead is able to sing as
it sails around its miniature sea.

00:10:57.650 --> 00:11:02.930
It’s not every day that an SCP has a happy
ending, but this seems to be one of those

00:11:02.930 --> 00:11:04.380
rare cases.

00:11:04.380 --> 00:11:09.560
The ghost ship sightings have ceased altogether,
the case has been closed, and the anomaly

00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:10.930
is at rest.

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:16.090
It’s not hard to imagine that the figurehead
is enjoying its new, peaceful life, no longer

00:11:16.090 --> 00:11:19.890
forced to sink ships or be used as a weapon
of war.

00:11:19.890 --> 00:11:23.820
Instead, it can simply float, and sing its
songs.

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:29.540
Now go check out “SCP-1861 - The Crew of
the HMS Wintersheimer” and “A Sea Snail's

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:35.649
Fantasy SCP-1867 - A Gentleman” for more
horrors and oddities of the high seas!

